<task id="002" project="messaging-communication-app-r1">
  <metadata>
    <title>Phase-0: decide private-thread grant mechanism (`GrantAccess` vs `sprk_externalrecordaccess`)</title>
    <phase>W0 — Phase 0 Verification + Foundation</phase>
    <status>not-started</status>
    <estimated-hours>5</estimated-hours>
    <dependencies>none</dependencies>
    <blocks>042</blocks>
    <tags>spike, security</tags>
    <rigor-hint>STANDARD</rigor-hint>
    <rigor-reason>Security-sensitive decision spike — no code, no build. Resolves the ONE open architectural question (design §5, §14.5). Output is a decision record that unblocks the highest-risk task (042 privacy enforcement). STANDARD per §8 (investigation/decision), but the decision itself is security-sensitive → escalate per root §6 if the two options materially diverge on correctness.</rigor-reason>
    <parallel-group>W0-A</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>true</parallel-safe>
    <fr-refs>—</fr-refs>
    <model-tier>sonnet</model-tier>
    <effort>high</effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>
    Phase-0 verification of the single open architectural question in R1 (design §5, §14.5, spec Unresolved Questions): **how do we grant a specific set of named participants access to a PRIVATE communication thread?** The access model is otherwise locked — OPEN (record-anchored) threads derive membership from `MembershipResolverService` (ADR-034); this spike resolves ONLY the *private*-thread grant primitive.

    Two candidate mechanisms, both already present in the codebase; pick one with evidence:

    - **(A) Dataverse `GrantAccess` (Principal-Object-Access / POA)** — the platform-native per-record sharing primitive (the `PlaybookSharingService` precedent uses it). Grants a principal read/append access directly on the thread (and/or message) record; Dataverse security composes it with the caller's base access.
    - **(B) `sprk_externalrecordaccess` overlay** — a Spaarke-owned overlay table (the `GrantExternalAccessEndpoint` precedent) that records grants as rows the BFF query-filter honours, rather than relying on POA.

    Inspect the two precedents in the actual code — `PlaybookSharingService` (GrantAccess path) and `GrantExternalAccessEndpoint` / `sprk_externalrecordaccess` (overlay path) — and compare on: correctness of enforcement (does the BFF thread-read filter in task 042/050 honour it cleanly?), composition with ADR-034-derived open membership, revocation + point-forward privacy-switch semantics (design §5 D-04), auditability, ACS-membership-reconcile compatibility (design §4/§8.4 — ACS membership is a projection of whichever grant model wins), and internal-vs-external participant reach (R1 internal; R2 BYOI external).

    This is a **decision spike, no code**. Output the decision + rationale + the rejected alternative to `notes/spikes/002-private-grant-decision.md`. Task 042 (privacy/internal-only/privilege enforcement) consumes this decision directly.
  </prompt>

  <role>
    Spaarke security architect for the Communication subsystem. Authoritative on Dataverse record security (POA / `GrantAccess`), the `sprk_externalrecordaccess` overlay, `MembershipResolverService` (ADR-034), and the BFF query-filter enforcement pattern. Security-sensitive posture (root §6) — surface correctness risks explicitly rather than defaulting silently.
  </role>

  <goal>
    A written decision record at `notes/spikes/002-private-grant-decision.md` that names the chosen private-thread grant mechanism (A `GrantAccess` or B `sprk_externalrecordaccess`), gives evidence-backed rationale across the comparison axes, documents the rejected alternative, and specifies exactly how task 042's BFF query-filter + task 041's ACS-membership reconcile must consume it. The one open architectural question of design §5 is closed; task 042 can be authored/executed against a locked mechanism.
  </goal>

  <context>
    <background>
      Design §5 locks the access boundary to the Dataverse record and locks OPEN-thread membership to ADR-034. The ONLY unresolved detail is the private-grant primitive — design §14.5 and spec Unresolved Questions both flag it as a Phase-0 verification (not a design fork). Getting it wrong is the highest-consequence error in R1: task 042 is the security-sensitive enforcement layer (NFR-06), and ACS membership is a *reconciled projection* of whatever grant model wins (design §4). A grant model the BFF filter cannot cleanly honour, or that has weak revocation / point-forward semantics, would leak privileged content — the concrete cost-of-doing-nothing named by NFR-06.

      §11 Component Justification: this task introduces NO new component — it CHOOSES between two EXISTING precedents (extension over new, per §11). The whole point is to avoid inventing a third access mechanism (design §5 "No new authorization engine").
    </background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file>src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/**/PlaybookSharingService.cs (GrantAccess / POA precedent — locate via grep)</file>
      <file>src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/**/GrantExternalAccessEndpoint*.cs (overlay precedent — locate via grep)</file>
      <file>src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/**/*externalrecordaccess* (the `sprk_externalrecordaccess` overlay usage — locate via grep)</file>
      <file>src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/**/MembershipResolver* (ADR-034 open-membership derivation, for composition)</file>
      <file>projects/messaging-communication-app-r1/design.md (§5 privacy/access, §4 membership sync, §8.4 ACS reconcile)</file>
    </relevant-files>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="project">Decision spike — NO code, NO build, NO schema change. Output is a markdown decision record only.</constraint>
    <constraint source="design §5">MUST NOT propose a new authorization engine or a third access mechanism. Choose between the two named existing precedents; open membership stays on ADR-034.</constraint>
    <constraint source="root §6 (security-sensitive)">If the two options materially diverge on enforcement correctness or revocation safety, surface it as a 🔔 Human Input Required escalation with a recommendation — do NOT pick silently.</constraint>
    <constraint source="design §5 D-04">The chosen mechanism MUST support point-forward privacy (opening a thread exposes only messages from that point forward; prior private messages stay restricted) and auditable revocation.</constraint>
    <constraint source="design §4 / §8.4">The chosen mechanism MUST be reconcilable to ACS thread membership (ACS membership is a projection of Dataverse-derived access — never a second source of truth).</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-034">Private grant COMPOSES WITH ADR-034 open membership; document how the BFF filter unions the two without double-counting or privilege leak.</constraint>
  </constraints>

